speechtext:
If I am permitted now to proceed, I would say that if the hon. junior member for West Toronto were in iiis seat, I would tell him that he had better clear his skirts in connection with the Calgary and Edmonton Railway, and the Qu'Appelle, Long Lake and Saskatchewan Railway, before he attempts to throw an aspersion on anybody else, no matter what their conduct may have been, with regard to discrediting the securities of this country in the money markets of the old country. Now, what are the facts connected with these transactions ? Parliament voted more than $22,000 per mile in cash guarantees and land grants in aid of these roads; but, according to the statement which comes to us from the opposition, it was found impossible, even with this enormous aid, to secure their construction. Ten years after the contract for the construction of the Canadian Pacific Railway had been made, signed, sealed and delivered, five years after that construction had been completed, so depressed were conditions in the Northwest Territories, that with cash and land guarantees which the government had granted to aid in the construction of railroads in that section of country to the extent of $22,000 per mile, our hon. friends confess that it was .impossible to secure Mr. SCOTT.
their construction. The late Sir John Macdonald, then Prime Minister of this country -so we have been told by hon. gentlemen opposite, including the hon. junior member for West Toronto-was at his wits' end about this thing ; and he said that the one man in the whole Dominion of Canada whom he considered big enough and shrewd enough as a financier and successful operator to accomplish it was the gentleman who now represents West Toronto in this House. Sir John Macdonald sent for this gentleman and asked him to sacrifice his own interests and go to the money markets of the old country, and see whether, with $22,000 per mile of guarantees from the parliament of Canada, he could not succeed in floating the enterprises in the money markets of the old land. The amount that was required to construct the roads was a matter of $6,283,162. Now, what did the hon. gentleman do ? As I have stated, with a practical guarantee of $22,000 per mile and with a further practical guarantee of the Canadian Pacific Railway for the bonds, he sold bonds to the vafue of $10,000,000. getting cash in return to the extent of $9,283,653. What I have stated are virtually the facts as shown in the public records, and which enabled me iast year to state that Mr. Osier and his associates had got towards these roads :
In cash guarantees from parliament, $3,200,000 ;
In lands worth at least $9,000,000 ;
In proceeds of bonds, $9,289,653 ;
By the issue of stock, $1,201,000 ;
Making a total of $22,684,653 ; to build roads whose total cost, as shown by the records in the Department of Railways and Canals was only $6,283,162.
As I stated last year, this left a margin to Mr. Osier and his associates of no less than $16,401,491, or a little rake-off amounting to sixteen and a half million dollars. But the hon. and junior member for West Toronto (Mr. Osier),-who did not happen to be in his seat at that time,-as he is not this afternoon and as he is very freiquentily not-came up about four days later with an explanation, in which he took exception to the statements I made. So that there may be no misunderstanding, I would ask you. Mr. Speaker, if I am privileged to quote from the debates of last year.
